<!--Post: 9426--><!--Time: 1109521064-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9426"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 27, 2005 - 8:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#40;OK, one more post&#41; <BR> <BR>Chanel 3 says that a cd-rom was in one of the later packages and a detective re-formatted it and got the name &#34;Dennis Rader&#34;. <BR> <BR>My forensic computer person says that a scanned image starts as a &#34;tiff&#34; file and then is ofteg converted to another format such as &#34;jpeg&#34; <BR> <BR>When the image is created, by default, the imaging software imbeds data in the file which often includes the registered owner of the software.  This is for use in copywrite disputes because it shows who initially created the image.  The data is not usually visible, and most people &#40;me included&#41; don&#39;t know it&#39;s there. <BR> <BR>Once converted to &#34;Jpeg&#34; the data is compressed and cannot be seen or read.   <BR> <BR>If you take a &#34;jpeg&#34; and re-format it as a &#34;tiff&#34; the data comes back and can be read with appropriate software. <BR> <BR>Oops! <BR> <BR>Don&#39;t you just hate it when that happens? <BR> <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 9426-->
<!--Post: 9435--><!--Time: 1109524446-->
